    "body": "`format()` falls back to automatic unit selection for an unrecognized `options.unit` (for example, `bb`, also covered by the existing custom-unit test). However, `constructor` and `__proto__` bypass that fallback and produce a `NaN` string.\n\n### Reproduction\n\n```sh\nnpm install bytes@3.1.2\n```\n\n```js\nconst bytes = require('bytes');\n\nconsole.log(bytes.format(1024, {unit: 'KB'}));          // 1KB\nconsole.log(bytes.format(1024, {unit: 'bb'}));          // 1KB\nconsole.log(bytes.format(1024, {unit: 'constructor'})); // NaNconstructor\nconsole.log(bytes.format(1024, {unit: '__proto__'}));   // NaN__proto__\n```\n\n### Expected\n\nThe last two calls should use the same unknown-unit fallback as `bb` and return `1KB`.\n\n### Cause\n\nThe unit check uses `map[unit.toLowerCase()]` on a normal object. The inherited `constructor` function and `__proto__` object are truthy, so they are accepted as conversion factors. Dividing the byte count by either produces `NaN`.\n\nChecking for an own property when validating the unit would avoid accepting those inherited properties.\n\nThis is an edge case for invalid custom unit strings; ordinary supported units are unaffected. No prototype modification is needed to reproduce it.\n\n### Verification\n\n- Reproduced with the published `bytes@3.1.2` package on Node.js v24.8.0 / macOS.\n- Also reproduced on current `master`, commit `9ddc13b6c66e0cb293616fba246e05db4b6cef4d`.\n- The existing test suite passes: 30 tests. Its custom-unit test checks the `bb` fallback but not inherited property names.\n- Reviewed the repository's existing issues and pull requests for duplicates.\n\nFound during an AI-assisted code review; the examples above were executed locally.\n",
