    "body": "`bytes@3.1.2`, Node v22.23.3:\n\n```js\nconst bytes = require('bytes');\n\nbytes.parse('1.5.2');    // => 1\nbytes.parse('1e3kb');    // => 1\nbytes.parse('1,5kb');    // => 1\nbytes.parse('0.1.2');    // => 0\nbytes.parse(' 5mb ');    // => 5   — leading/trailing space drops the unit\nbytes.parse('5mb ');     // => 5\n```\n\nAll of these are malformed, so I'd expect `null` — the README says the function returns \"null upon error\", and inputs without leading digits do behave that way (`bytes.parse('not a size')` → `null`).\n\nThe cause is the fallback in `index.js`:\n\n```js\nif (!results) {\n  // Nothing could be extracted from the given string\n  floatValue = parseInt(val, 10);\n  unit = 'b'\n}\n```\n\nWhen the anchored `number + unit` regex doesn't match, `parseInt(val, 10)` keeps any leading digits and silently discards the rest. So a size limit read from a config file (`'5mb '` with a stray trailing space, `'1.5.2'` from a typo) is turned into a confidently wrong byte count (5 bytes / 1 byte) instead of an error the caller can catch.\n\nNote that plain numeric strings keep working either way (`bytes.parse('1024')` → `1024`), so the fallback isn't needed for the documented \"no unit means bytes\" case — the regex already covers it.\n\nSuggestion: return `null` when the regex doesn't match (or restrict the fallback to purely numeric strings).",
